# The address the OTA response hands the device. Left unset, the bridge picks a
# LAN address itself; set it when that pick is wrong (a second adapter, a VPN).
#BRIDGE_PUBLIC_HOST=192.168.1.100

# Where the two servers listen. These are the ports the flashed device expects.
#OTA_PORT=8003
#WS_PORT=8000

# The secret every device's token is derived from. REQUIRED, at least 32
# characters, and never reused between deployments: whoever holds it can connect
# as any device on the allowlist. It is the one secret this service keeps off
# hardware. Generate one with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
#BRIDGE_DEVICE_SECRET=

# Where adaptive-interview-api is, as a base URL. REQUIRED, and with no default,
# unlike the ports above: those are addresses this machine serves on, but this
# one belongs to a deployment, and the only obvious default is localhost — right
# on one machine and silently wrong on every other, where the bridge would come up
# looking healthy and reach nothing. Any trailing slash is dropped when this is
# read, so leave it off or leave it on, whichever you prefer.
#BRIDGE_PLATFORM_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4000

# The platform's own credential, as the platform has it in its .env. REQUIRED.
# This is the value the bridge calls adaptive-interview-api with, server to
# server, on a device's behalf — and the one credential a device must never hold:
# a gadget authenticates to this bridge with the token the OTA endpoint issues it,
# and with nothing else. Copy it from adaptive-interview-api's .env, and never
# onto hardware. There is no length rule: it is the platform's token, carried
# unchanged, and the platform compares it byte for byte.
#API_AUTH_TOKEN=

# The devices this bridge will provision, as their Device-Id (a MAC address).
# Separators and case do not matter, so b8:1f:3f:4a:9b:01 and B8-1F-3F-4A-9B-01
# name the same device. EMPTY MEANS NOBODY: a device is allowed because it is
# named here, and every other device is refused at the OTA endpoint and at the
# socket.
#BRIDGE_ALLOWED_DEVICES=b8:1f:3f:4a:9b:01

# Which persona each device speaks as, as DEVICE=PERSONA, one per device named
# above. Separators and case are read the same way the allowlist reads them, so
# the two lists can be copied from the same place. The two must describe the same
# devices in both directions: a device that may connect and has no persona could
# never be answered, and a binding for a device the allowlist does not name is
# configuration for something that can never connect. Either is refused at start.
# The persona identifier is the catalog's own id (GET /api/personas) — the
# bridge holds no personas of its own, and a gadget speaks as exactly one.
#BRIDGE_DEVICE_PERSONAS=b8:1f:3f:4a:9b:01=language-partner

# The binary framing version. The firmware negotiates one of 3, 2, 1.
#BRIDGE_FRAMING=3

# What the server hello declares it will send. 24000 is the platform's measured
# Kokoro output rate and a legal Opus rate; the firmware defaults to the same, so
# nothing resamples.
#BRIDGE_SERVER_RATE=24000
#BRIDGE_FRAME_MS=60

# THE ENDPOINTER — how the bridge decides the person has stopped speaking. The
# firmware sends no `listen stop`, so nothing else says (design.md D9), and the
# defaults below are all measurements rather than preferences. Leave them unset
# unless a deployment's room is unusual enough to justify a figure of your own.

# The gate: the quietest frame that counts at all, in RMS, and how far above the
# room's own estimated floor a frame must be. They keep the quiet out; they do not
# pick the speaker, because loudness cannot — this board is half-duplex with no echo
# cancellation, and in the corpus a trigger the platform answered as noise reached
# 20395 RMS while a real sentence sat at 10875.
#BRIDGE_VAD_MIN_RMS=7500
#BRIDGE_VAD_FLOOR_RATIO=2.5

# How much silence after speech closes a turn, and how much speech a window must
# hold before it may close at all. The silence threshold is what the person waits
# out after their last word — it is spent on every turn by construction. The speech
# minimum is what keeps a door, a chair or the case being set down from being a turn,
# and 500 ms is measured, not chosen: over the 37 turns on record, every trigger the
# platform answered as noise needed 8 frames or fewer at 60 ms and every one it
# answered with a full sentence needed 9 or more. Below it a three-frame knock
# becomes a transcript, a reply and audio out of the gadget's speaker.
#BRIDGE_VAD_SILENCE_MS=900
#BRIDGE_VAD_MIN_SPEECH_MS=500

# The window speech is counted over. It must exceed BRIDGE_VAD_SILENCE_MS plus
# BRIDGE_VAD_MIN_SPEECH_MS, and the bridge refuses to start otherwise: speech that
# ages out of the window before the silence threshold is reached is a gadget that
# never answers at all, silently.
#BRIDGE_VAD_SPEECH_WINDOW_MS=3000

# The backstop — the longest a turn may run before it is closed anyway, for a person
# who never stops — and the other end, how long a window may hold no speech at all
# before it is given up on. The giveup is the line the log writes when a transient
# arrives in a room where nobody spoke.
#BRIDGE_MAX_TURN_MS=20000
#BRIDGE_VAD_NO_SPEECH_MS=15000

# How much audio before the first voiced frame is kept, so a soft onset is not
# clipped off the utterance; and how long after the bridge's own audio the device's
# microphone is distrusted, since the device reopens it before its speaker stops
# (D10). The guard is dropped when the platform produces a reply slower than
# realtime, where it would be spent before it was armed (D16).
#BRIDGE_ONSET_LEAD_MS=300
#BRIDGE_DRAIN_GUARD_MS=400

# How long a session may hold nobody before the bridge ends it, in ms. `0` turns the
# window off and holds a session open for as long as the device keeps it open — how
# this bridge behaved before the window existed, and the way back to that.
#
# Ending a session is what leaves the device Idle: microphone closed, wake word armed
# again, and the one state from which the firmware's own power management can run at
# all. A session held open is a gadget that never rests — and, in the other direction,
# a person who never needs the wake word. 60000 is the vendor server's own window,
# measured on 2026-10-10 at 60 s from the session hello, so adopting it changes nothing
# this deployment had not already been living with. See docs/vendor-server.md.
#BRIDGE_SESSION_IDLE_MS=60000

# Where this bridge's own music is: a directory of audio files it plays through the gadget's
# speaker when asked. Left unset there is no music at all, and that is not a degraded mode — the
# two commands ("phát nhạc", "dừng nhạc") go unrecognised and fall through as ordinary utterances,
# and no tool of the bridge's own is offered to a model, so a turn is asked for exactly as it was
# before any of this existed.
#
# Read when it is asked rather than at start, so a file dropped in while the bridge runs is
# playable, and a directory somebody deleted is "nothing to play" rather than a bridge that will
# not boot.
#BRIDGE_MUSIC_DIR=/srv/music

# The language this gadget is set to, as `english` or `vietnamese`. Declared on
# every turn the bridge sends, because the platform decodes the utterance in the
# language the turn names rather than detecting one, and reads an absent value as
# English. It is a property of the deployment, not of the audio: a Vietnamese
# gadget whose turns omitted this would come back in a foreign script while the
# bridge looked healthy. Defaults to english.
#BRIDGE_LANGUAGE=english
